WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.578
JENNY BROWN: Powering your job
search with the Cloud Talent

00:00:02.578 --> 00:00:04.650
Solution job search API
lets you start getting

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.583
quality results immediately.

00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:09.000
However, there are plenty of
ways to tweak search requests

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.928
to match any business needs.

00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:12.720
Let's look at some
configuration parameters

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.360
you can apply and test when
making a search request.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.252
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.220
To make the biggest impact
on the number and relevance

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.650
of jobs returned when
someone does a search, let's

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.470
look at two parameters you can
configure when making this API

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:28.640
search request--

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.000
enableBroadening and
disableKeywordMatch.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.370
enablebroadening does
exactly what it sounds like.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.860
It broadens the search
results that get returned.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.870
It does this by relaxing
the restrictions

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.430
on location and job categories.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.310
This is a binary
property for the search,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.290
so it's either true or
false and defaults to false

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.840
if you don't set it yourself.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.750
Setting it to true will give
more results to a search query

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.940
but may start to return
extended results that

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.850
aren't an exact match for
what the user searched for.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:56.690
Don't worry.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.750
The most relevant jobs
will still appear first.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.030
The next property is
disableKeywordMatch,

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.770
which is another
binary property that

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.260
tells the search request
to return additional jobs

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.000
through keyword matching.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.770
When set to false,
additional job

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.590
matches that aren't deemed
relevant by the ML algorithm

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:15.950
but do match the search
query string get returned.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.620
Just like with enableBroadening,
disableKeywordMatch

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.050
defaults to false, and
any additional results

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.702
will appear lower than
those most relevant.

00:01:23.702 --> 00:01:25.160
Let's look at what
happens when you

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.250
use enableBroadening and
disableKeywordMatch together.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.050
This quick table
gives a general idea

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.910
of what users will
see in a search result

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.620
based on these parameters.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.510
Again, the most
relevant search results

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.130
are always returned first.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.530
Without changing anything,
a default search request

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.110
has enableBroadening
and disableKeywordMatch

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.410
set to false.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:44.960
This leads to the
most relevant jobs

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.170
being followed by any jobs
that match the keyword search.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.690
If you change
disableKeywordMatch to be true

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.060
but leave
enableBroadening as false,

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.070
your search results will
include only machine

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.530
learning recommended results
since Keyword Matching

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.050
and Broadening have
both been turned off.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.110
This means only the most
relevant jobs are displayed,

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.570
but it's also the configuration
with the fewest number

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.310
of jobs returned.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.860
Now if you flip these
and have enableBroadening

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.930
set to true and
disableKeywordMatch set

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.940
to false, you'll get
the maximum number

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.680
of jobs returned for a search.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.260
The most relevant jobs will
be followed by keyword matched

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.700
and expanded query jobs.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.700
Finally, if both
disableKeywordMatch and

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.940
enableBroadening or set
to true, search queries

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.320
will turn the most relevant
jobs followed by only

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.310
the expanded query results.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.155
So you can see how
these two properties can

00:02:31.155 --> 00:02:33.530
be used to influence jobs that
get returned from a search

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:34.220
query.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.117
There's no right or
wrong configuration.

00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:37.700
It just depends on
what makes the most

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.547
sense for your business case.

00:02:39.547 --> 00:02:41.630
We recommend trying a few
different configurations

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.468
to see which works best
for you and your users.

00:02:44.468 --> 00:02:46.760
Now that we've covered those
two parameters, let's look

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.550
at two more options you can use
to further refine the jobs that

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:51.380
show up in your user searches.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.690
We'll look at featured
jobs and custom ranking

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.110
in the next video.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.690
Thanks for watching
and remember,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.280
when looking for talent, it's OK
to keep your head in the cloud.

